<h2>A landlocked market with genuine banana demand</h2>
<p>Uzbekistan is doubly landlocked — neither it nor its immediate neighbours have direct sea access — which makes it a structurally different market from most of the countries in our guide. Despite that, the trade data we've verified shows a genuinely significant volume of Indian banana moving into Uzbekistan, reflecting established regional trade relationships built around Central Asia's demand for imported fresh fruit.</p>

<h2>Routing is multimodal, not a straightforward port call</h2>
<p>Because there's no direct sea route, freight to Uzbekistan typically moves by sea to a port such as Bandar Abbas in Iran and then overland, or via rail corridors through Central Asia. That makes transit time meaningfully longer and more routing-dependent than our sea-freight markets — we confirm current realistic routing and timing at the time of your order rather than quoting a fixed figure.</p>

